# Vulnerability details

## Impact

In `LybraPeUSDVaultBase` and in `LybraEUSDVaultBase`, on `liquidation()`, provider allowance is only checked to be >0. For PeUSD, this makes unchecked burn from the provider address possible if the allowance is bigger than fees accrued. For EUSD, unchecked burn is possible if allowance is > 1 wei.

## Impact Details

`LybraPeUSDVaultBase` case. On this line, liquidation checks for an allowance of >0 tokens from provider. 

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-lybra/blob/5d70170f2c68dbd3f7b8c0c8fd6b0b2218784ea6/contracts/lybra/pools/base/LybraPeUSDVaultBase.sol#L131

This can be escalated if provider has allowed just enough tokens to pay fees, but not enough to do the liquidation: `repay()` will first call `transferFrom`, which checks for allowance from the provider. But then it will call `burn()`, which does not check for, nor deduct allowance. So more tokens will be removed from the provider's account than he has allowed for.

In the EUSD case, no transferFrom is called, therefore any allowance > 1 wei allows for unchecked burn.

